  <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Lesson 0008 · The Dashboard Tool</p>
  <h1>The Dashboard You Just Ran</h1>
  <p class="why"><b>Why this matters:</b> you drove <code>app/dashboard.py</code> live — clicked
  <b>Run pipeline</b>, loaded the volume panel, watched the microstructure expander warn about a missing
  build. Every one of those behaviors comes from <b>one Streamlit mechanic</b> you haven't been taught yet:
  the whole script <em>re-runs top to bottom on every click</em>. Understand that, and the dashboard's design
  stops looking clever-but-mysterious and starts looking obvious.</p>

  <div class="bus"></div>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <h2><span class="n">01</span> The concept: Streamlit has no callbacks — it has reruns</h2>

  <p class="lead">A traditional GUI toolkit (Tkinter, PyQt, a browser SPA) builds a <b>persistent object
  graph</b> once, then calls a small callback function when you click a button — everything else on screen
  stays exactly as it was. Streamlit does something stranger: <b>every single interaction — every click,
  every slider drag, every text entry — throws away the whole page and re-executes your entire Python script
  from line 1</b>, top to bottom, as a fresh script run.</p>

  <div class="runtime">
    <div class="col trad">
      <h4>Traditional GUI (event callbacks)</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Build the window + widgets <b>once</b>, at startup.</li>
        <li>Register a callback function per button.</li>
        <li>User clicks → <b>only that callback runs</b>.</li>
        <li>Everything else on screen is untouched — it was never re-created.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="col st">
      <h4>Streamlit (script reruns)</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>User clicks / drags / types <b>anything</b>, anywhere on the page.</li>
        <li>Streamlit re-executes <code>dashboard.py</code> <b>from the top</b>, in full.</li>
        <li>Every widget call (<code>st.button</code>, <code>st.slider</code>…) runs again and repaints itself.</li>
        <li>Only <code>st.session_state</code> survives from the <em>previous</em> run — everything else is rebuilt fresh.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>This single fact explains nearly everything non-obvious in <code>dashboard.py</code>: why results are
  stashed in <code>st.session_state</code> instead of a plain variable, why the Oil Volume panel stays open
  after you click other widgets, and why the microstructure panel doesn't re-parse a million-tick file every
  time you change the λ-window dropdown. Those aren't extra features — they're <b>workarounds for the rerun
  model</b>, and once you see the pattern you'll recognize it everywhere in Streamlit code.</p>

  <div class="progress-controls"><button class="mark" data-mark>◻ mark section read</button></div>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <h2><span class="n">02</span> Minimal example: the trap, and the fix</h2>
  <p>Here's the mistake every Streamlit beginner makes first, stripped to five lines — followed by the fix
  <code>dashboard.py</code> actually uses.</p>

<pre><span class="c"># BROKEN: looks like a counter, but never counts past 1.</span>
<span class="k">import</span> streamlit <span class="k">as</span> st

count = <span class="n">0</span>                              <span class="c"># plain local variable</span>
<span class="k">if</span> st.button(<span class="s">"+1"</span>):                    <span class="c"># clicking reruns the WHOLE script</span>
    count += <span class="n">1</span>                          <span class="c"># count is reset to 0 above, THEN incremented -> always 1</span>
st.write(count)

<span class="c"># FIXED: state that must outlive a rerun goes in st.session_state.</span>
<span class="k">if</span> <span class="s">"count"</span> <span class="k">not in</span> st.session_state:
    st.session_state[<span class="s">"count"</span>] = <span class="n">0</span>          <span class="c"># only runs on the very first load</span>
<span class="k">if</span> st.button(<span class="s">"+1"</span>):
    st.session_state[<span class="s">"count"</span>] += <span class="n">1</span>          <span class="c"># survives every future rerun</span>
st.write(st.session_state[<span class="s">"count"</span>])</pre>

  <p class="muted">Every "why is this cached in <code>session_state</code>?" question below has the exact
  same answer as this five-line example — just applied to a 2-second EIA fetch or a 3-second ITCH parse
  instead of an integer.</p>

  <div class="progress-controls"><button class="mark" data-mark>◻ mark section read</button></div>

