  <h2><span class="n">03</span> In this project: three real reruns, walked through</h2>

  <h3>3.1 — Why the pipeline result doesn't vanish when you touch another widget</h3>
  <p>The <b>▶ Run pipeline</b> button only drives one small block near the bottom of the file
  (<code>app/dashboard.py:318-324</code>):</p>
<pre><span class="k">if</span> run:
    cfg = RunConfig(start=start, end=end, return_kind=return_kind, max_lag=max_lag, …)
    _run(cfg)   <span class="c"># drains run_pipeline(cfg) and writes the final PipelineResult into session_state</span></pre>
  <p>Inside <code>_run</code> (<code>dashboard.py:303-315</code>), the last line is the one that matters:</p>
<pre>st.session_state[<span class="s">"result"</span>] = final   <span class="c"># dashboard.py:315 — NOT a local variable</span></pre>
  <p>Then, unconditionally, <b>every single rerun</b> — whether triggered by the Run button or by you idly
  dragging the "Rolling window" slider — reads it back (<code>dashboard.py:326</code>):</p>
<pre>result = st.session_state.get(<span class="s">"result"</span>)
<span class="k">if</span> result <span class="k">is not</span> <span class="k">None</span> <span class="k">and</span> result.leadlag <span class="k">is not</span> <span class="k">None</span>:
    _render_results(result)   <span class="c"># repaints the SAME cached result on every rerun until you click Run again</span></pre>
  <p>That's why, when you ran the pipeline from cache and then (hypothetically) nudged another control, the
  Results section wouldn't blank out — it isn't "live," it's a snapshot sitting in <code>session_state</code>
  that every rerun faithfully redraws until a new <b>Run pipeline</b> click overwrites it.</p>

  <h3>3.2 — The panel that stays open after its own button "disappears"</h3>
  <p>The Oil Size / Volume panel has the same trick, one line different (<code>dashboard.py:337-340</code>):</p>
<pre><span class="k">if</span> run_volume <span class="k">or</span> st.session_state.get(<span class="s">"volume_loaded"</span>):
    st.session_state[<span class="s">"volume_loaded"</span>] = <span class="k">True</span>
    st.divider()
    _render_oil_volume(eia_key=eia_key, start=start, end=end)</pre>
  <p><code>run_volume</code> is <code>True</code> for exactly <b>one rerun</b> — the one triggered by the click
  itself; on the next rerun it's back to <code>False</code> (Streamlit re-evaluates <code>st.button(...)</code>
  fresh every time). Without the <code>or st.session_state.get("volume_loaded")</code> half, the panel would
  flicker open for one instant and vanish the moment you touched anything else. The flag is what makes "loaded"
  sticky.</p>

  <h3>3.3 — Not re-parsing 1.5 million ticks because you changed a dropdown</h3>
  <p>The microstructure expander is the most expensive path in the app — parsing an ITCH day through the C++
  engine takes real seconds. Naively, changing the "Kyle's λ window" selectbox would rerun the whole script and
  re-parse the file just to recompute λ. The guard (<code>dashboard.py:370-375</code>) stops that:</p>
<pre><span class="k">if</span> st.session_state.get(<span class="s">"micro_path"</span>) != path:
    <span class="k">with</span> st.spinner(<span class="s">f"Parsing {os.path.basename(path)} (C++ engine)…"</span>):
        events = load_micro_events(path, AAPL_GOLDEN_MIN_RAW, AAPL_GOLDEN_MAX_RAW)  <span class="c"># slow: full C++ parse</span>
    st.session_state[<span class="s">"micro_path"</span>] = path
    st.session_state[<span class="s">"micro_events"</span>] = events
events = st.session_state[<span class="s">"micro_events"</span>]  <span class="c"># cheap: dict lookup, every rerun after the first</span></pre>
  <p>The λ-window selectbox change still reruns the <em>entire script</em> — that part never stops — but the
  <code>if</code> guard means the expensive C++ parse only executes once per file path; every subsequent rerun
  (window changed, unrelated sidebar slider touched, anything) just reads the cached DataFrame back out.</p>

  <div class="warnbox"><b>A bug you watched happen live.</b> When you ran the app, the microstructure expander
  showed <em>"signals_cpp extension not built"</em> — even though the extension <b>is</b> compiled. The cause
  has nothing to do with Streamlit's rerun model; it's a plain import-path bug in
  <code>src/oil_alpha/microstructure/events.py</code>:
  <pre><span class="k">try</span>:
    <span class="k">import</span> signals_cpp <span class="k">as</span> _cpp     <span class="c"># bare import — only works if the .so is directly importable</span>
    USING_CPP = <span class="k">True</span>
<span class="k">except</span> ImportError:
    USING_CPP = <span class="k">False</span>              <span class="c"># silently "not built", even when it is</span></pre>
  Compare <code>src/oil_alpha/microstructure/mm_analysis.py</code>, which tries the bare import <em>then</em>
  falls back to <code>from oil_alpha import signals_cpp</code> — the layout this editable install actually
  uses. That's why the MM-sim analysis path works and this one doesn't: one file has the fallback, the other
  doesn't. Same lesson as 0001's ABI-tag discussion — a compiled extension being importable depends entirely on
  <em>how</em> you import it, not just whether it was built.</div>

