  <h2><span class="n">05</span> Often confused with</h2>
  <div class="vs">
    <div class="col a">
      <h4>Event-callback GUIs (Tkinter, PyQt, Qt block builder)</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Widgets and their state are built <b>once</b> and live for the whole session.</li>
        <li>A click runs <b>only its own callback</b>; unrelated UI is provably untouched.</li>
        <li>State lives in ordinary object attributes — no special "surviving" container needed.</li>
        <li>Caching an expensive result is the <em>default</em>, not something you have to opt into.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="col b">
      <h4>Streamlit's rerun model</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>The <b>entire script</b> re-executes top to bottom on every interaction, no exceptions.</li>
        <li>There is no "only this callback ran" — everything on the page re-evaluates.</li>
        <li>Only <code>st.session_state</code> (a dict) survives between runs; plain variables reset.</li>
        <li>Caching (session_state guards, or <code>@st.cache_data</code>) is something <b>you</b> add, or you re-do expensive work on every click.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">They <b>share</b> the goal — react to user input and update the screen. They <b>diverge</b>
  on the unit of re-execution: a callback runs a few lines; a Streamlit rerun re-runs the whole program. Once
  you expect that, the <code>session_state</code> guards scattered through <code>dashboard.py</code> stop
  looking like defensive clutter and start looking like the one thing standing between you and re-parsing a
  1.5-million-row file on every keystroke.</p>

  <div class="progress-controls"><button class="mark" data-mark>◻ mark section read</button></div>

