  <h2><span class="n">06</span> Practice — click an answer</h2>
  <p class="muted">Options shuffle on every load; there is no "always the first one." Each choice explains itself.</p>

  <div id="quiz">
    <div class="question" data-question>
      <span class="qtag">Q1 · rerun model</span>
      <p>You click the "Rolling window" slider (no pipeline click). What runs?</p>
      <ul class="options">
        <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct — Streamlit has no partial reruns. The whole script executes again; it's just that `run` (from st.button) is False this time, so the `if run:` block is skipped and the OLD session_state result is redrawn.">The entire <code>dashboard.py</code> script top to bottom — but since <code>run</code> is False this time, the pipeline itself doesn't re-execute.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="Streamlit has no mechanism to run 'just the widget that changed' — there is no such partial-callback model here.">Only the code near the slider — the rest of the script is left alone.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="run_pipeline only runs inside the `if run:` block, which is gated on the button's own click, not on any other widget changing.">The pipeline reruns automatically because any widget change re-triggers `run_pipeline`.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="Nothing executes at all only if Streamlit weren't listening to widget events — but every widget interaction does trigger a script rerun.">Nothing — sliders don't trigger reruns, only buttons do.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
    </div>

    <div class="question" data-question>
      <span class="qtag">Q2 · session_state</span>
      <p>What would break if <code>dashboard.py:315</code>'s <code>st.session_state["result"] = final</code> were changed to a plain local variable <code>result = final</code>?</p>
      <ul class="options">
        <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right — a plain local variable is recreated fresh (as None/undefined) on every rerun. The Results section would vanish the instant you touched any other widget, since nothing would remember the last pipeline output." >The Results section would disappear the moment you touched any other widget, since a local variable doesn't survive the next rerun.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="The pipeline's own internal logic (run_pipeline, RunConfig) is unaffected by how the dashboard stores its output afterward.">The pipeline would compute the wrong lead-lag statistics.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="Nothing about session_state usage changes plotting behavior itself — the figures are built from `result`, whichever way it's stored, during the SAME rerun the pipeline just ran in.">The Plotly charts would fail to render entirely.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="Nothing would actually improve — losing session_state here only removes the ability to persist output across reruns.">Nothing — session_state and a local variable behave identically here.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
    </div>

    <div class="question" data-question>
      <span class="qtag">Q3 · caching pattern</span>
      <p>Why does <code>_render_microstructure</code> check <code>if st.session_state.get("micro_path") != path:</code> before parsing?</p>
      <ul class="options">
        <li data-correct="true" data-why="Exactly — the C++ ITCH parse is the most expensive operation in the app. Without the guard, changing the unrelated λ-window selectbox would re-trigger a full script rerun and re-parse the file from scratch every time.">To avoid re-running the expensive C++ ITCH parse on every rerun triggered by unrelated widgets, like the λ-window selectbox.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="signals_cpp is a compiled module cached by Python's import system regardless of this check; the guard is about not re-parsing DATA, not re-importing code.">To avoid re-importing the <code>signals_cpp</code> module, which is expensive to import.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="The guard doesn't change correctness of the OFI/microprice math — it only avoids redundant, identical recomputation of the same parse.">To ensure the OFI and microprice values stay mathematically correct across reruns.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="File existence is checked separately (os.path.exists) earlier in the function; this particular guard is purely a performance cache keyed on the path.">To check whether the ITCH file exists on disk before attempting to open it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
    </div>

    <div class="question" data-question>
      <span class="qtag">Q4 · the bug you saw</span>
      <p>Why did the Microstructure panel warn "extension not built" during your live run, even though <code>signals_cpp</code> was compiled?</p>
      <ul class="options">
        <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct — events.py does a bare `import signals_cpp`, which only succeeds if the .so is directly on the import path. In this editable-install layout it lives under oil_alpha/, so only `from oil_alpha import signals_cpp` succeeds — the pattern mm_analysis.py already uses as a fallback." >`events.py` uses a bare <code>import signals_cpp</code>, which fails in this editable-install layout; it needs a <code>from oil_alpha import signals_cpp</code> fallback like <code>mm_analysis.py</code> already has.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="The build genuinely succeeded — the compiled .so file exists and was confirmed importable via `from oil_alpha import signals_cpp` earlier in this session.">The C++ build genuinely failed and the extension doesn't actually exist.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="This has nothing to do with Streamlit's rerun/session_state model — it's a plain Python import-path issue, unrelated to reruns.">Streamlit's rerun model reset the import cache, so the module had to be re-imported and failed.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="The ITCH fixture file was present and used successfully elsewhere — the failure happens before any file is even opened, at the `import signals_cpp` line.">The AAPL ITCH fixture file was missing from the test machine.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
    </div>

    <div class="question" data-question>
      <span class="qtag">Q5 · often confused with</span>
      <p>What's the key difference between Streamlit's model and a traditional Tkinter/PyQt callback GUI?</p>
      <ul class="options">
        <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right — that's the core distinction this lesson opened with: Streamlit has no 'run just this callback' concept. Every interaction re-executes the full script; only session_state persists." >Streamlit re-runs the ENTIRE script on every interaction; a callback GUI runs only the specific handler tied to what you clicked.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="Streamlit apps are still ordinary Python and CAN use loops/conditionals freely — that's not what distinguishes the two models.">Streamlit doesn't allow loops or conditionals in app code, but Tkinter does.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both models render to a screen the user interacts with — that's not the distinguishing factor between them.">Tkinter renders to a native window while Streamlit renders to nothing visible.</li>
        <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both models are equally capable of holding state — the difference is WHERE it's safe to hold it (session_state vs. plain object attributes), not whether state exists at all.">Streamlit apps cannot hold any state at all, ever.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
    </div>
